\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When up, the two vehicle gates should look like 
\f1\b \strokec2 detached fragments of Eloise\'92s residence architecture
\f0\b0 \strokec2  rather than security barriers.\
Each rises from the roadside as a loose lattice of 
\f1\b \strokec2 slender, smoke-dark alloy tendrils
\f0\b0 \strokec2 , sweeping upward and inward in interlocking curves. The members do not form bars or a grille. They cross and separate in elongated triangular patterns, echoing the interwoven geometry of the house, with enough open space that the bush and road remain visible through them.\
The overall effect is light and slightly improbable: more like two pieces of architectural calligraphy standing across the road than gates. Their outer edges are anchored low beside each lane; their inner edges climb higher toward the road centre, so when both are up they almost\'97but do not quite\'97complete a single symmetrical figure.\
Nothing is square. Nothing looks load-bearing enough to be trusted. The alloy members taper as they rise, some narrowing almost to wires before thickening again where curves intersect. In changing light they pick up the same glassy, smoky reflections as the residence.\
At night, very fine light runs through selected lines of the lattice rather than outlining the whole structure. Red or green traffic signals remain separate and unambiguous; the gates themselves are decorative, not semaphore.\
The important visual qualities are:\
